  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nMidday satellite imagery depicted a continued clear sky over \nSW KS and adjoining areas, although widespread elevated smoke\naloft was present. Leeside cyclogenesis will deepen to near \n992 mb by 4 pm, which will increase south winds considerably\nthis afternoon. Gusts over 30 mph will be common, and the added\nmixing will facilitate much hotter temperatures over 100 at most\nlocations. Some record highs will be threatened, particularly at\nGCK (104/1987). Dewpoints will be restricted to the 50s all\nzones, making this a dry heat. As such, the heat index is\nexpected to be held below 105 all locations, although air\ntemperatures may reach 106-108 west of US 83 this afternoon. \n\n12z NAM forecasts thunderstorms to develop along an advancing\ncold front along the Colorado/Kansas border this evening, then\npropagate southeastward, entering the northwest zones at least\nto a limited extent overnight. Models disagree how far southeast\nany storms will advance, but with moisture and upper support\nlimited, the weaker/slower solutions are probably closer to\nreality. Some limited pops were maintained northwest zones\ntonight, with a low risk of 50-60 mph gusts given the hot and\ndry mixed boundary layer and inverted-v soundings. \n\nThe cold front will sag southward across SW KS Tuesday,\naccompanied by an increase in midlevel clouds and a northeast\nwind shift. A strong temperature gradient is expected by\nafternoon, with specific details determined by exact frontal\nplacement. NBM forecasts a 20 degree range across the CWA at\n4 pm Tuesday, ranging from 83 northwest Hamilton to 103\nsoutheast Barber. In between, highs will be shaved back to near\nnormal, in the lower 90s at DDC. The cooler air mass slides\nsouth through Wednesday morning, with sunrise temperatures in\nthe upper 50s and lower 60s common.\n\nWednesday and Thursday, the strong subtropical midlevel high is\nforecast to build over the Great Basin, well west of SW KS. As\nsuch, heat will remain in check at midweek, with 4 pm\ntemperatures near normal, in the upper 80s and lower 90s. \nThe resulting northwest flow aloft will leave the door open for\nsome needed rainfall opportunities, with NBM pops in the\nscattered/chance category late Wednesday, and now into the\nlikely category late Thursday. With the old frontal boundary\nmeandering around, and easterly upslope flow, moisture will have\nreturned by midweek, with dewpoints well into the 60s. With some\nCAPE and shear to work with, some marginally severe wind/hail\nrisk is evident Wednesday/Thursday. \n\nFriday through next Monday, rain chances end, and the heat \nrebuilds, as the high pressure ridge very gradually builds\neastward back onto the plains.",
